    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1087">

                <pb facs="1209" n="1199" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg1199"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00116 European War/1228: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-01-04T13:00:00+03:00">January 4, 1944—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 6:54 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>16. Reference Department’s 9, January 1, 7 p.m. Will keep Department fully
                    informed of future information we obtain regarding <pb facs="1210" n="1200"
                        xml:id="pg1200"/> atrocity trials.<anchor n="32" xml:id="d1087-f1"/> As I
                    understand your cable, it does not request me to take any steps against the
                    Soviet Government and I will do nothing more unless instructed.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1087-f1" n="32" xml:id="d1087-f1-content">
                <p>In telegram 92, January 12, 1944, the Ambassador sent a translation of an article
                    by the Soviet jurist Aron Naumovich Trainin entitled “The Criminal
                    Responsibility of the Hitlerite Criminals,” printed in <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >War and the Working Class</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Voyna i rabochy
                        klass</hi>) for January 1, 1944, wherein the Soviet attitude toward the
                    trial of captured war criminals was plainly stated (740.00116 European
                    War/1245).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
